// ===================== 5.3 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('5.3  The subject that is four lines long'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ **Make them count the two halves aloud, in words, in front of the room.** **The counting is the exercise: a writer who has said _twenty-three and four_ about their own sentence will not write another one like it that term.** **The worst sentence in most classes has a subject of about twenty words and a predicate of three**, and its writer chose it over a shorter version because it looked more careful.'));
out.push(A('**b** Make the reader wait: **2** and **4**. ⚠ **Not 1, 3 or 5.** **2 has nineteen words before _are_ and 4 has fifteen before _remains_.** **1 is the extraposed repair of 2 and the verb arrives at word four.** **3 is the full-stop repair and arrives at word three.** ⚠ **5 is the one worth four minutes**: _the question of whether the file copy exists has not been answered_ **has an eight-word subject, which is not a fault** — **the test is not length but whether the reader is holding new material, and _the question of whether the file copy exists_ is the thing the paragraph has been about.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — **it holds the long part back until the reader knows what is being done with it.**'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** ⚠ **Twenty-nine words before the verb, and everybody loses it at the second _that_** — **and there is nothing in the sentence a reader cannot follow: they simply have to hold three things with no idea what is coming.**   **2** **A reader can go back and a listener cannot**, and the person reading it aloud is usually not the writer, so the stress will also land in the wrong place.'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **The three repairs must all be written before any of them is chosen, and the class will want to stop at the first.** **Extraposition is the one they know and the one that looks professional.** **The colon and the list is the honest repair when the three things are three findings.** **And the full stop is right more often than anybody likes** — **and it is the one nobody reaches for, because at every level of this series from B1 onwards a long sentence has been treated as evidence of range.** ⚠ **Say that out loud: they have been rewarded for the fault since B1 and they are now being asked to undo it.** **The B2 writing bands reward complex structures and a C1 reader rewards a sentence they can take at one pass, and those two are not the same thing.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(A('**1** _It is not in dispute that the nine positions were never measured or that no certificate was issued._   **2** _Three things are not in dispute: the nine positions, the certificate and the file copy._   **3** _The nine positions were never measured. No certificate was issued. Neither is in dispute._   **4** ⚠ _It was accepted that the site had been used for storage, that no objection was received and that the road is adopted_ — **the three have to be held together, because the conclusion rests on all three.**   **5** _The question of whether the file copy exists has not been answered._ ⚠ **Fine as it is: the subject is eight words and every one of them is given.**'));
out.push(A('⚠ **Item 5 is the item that stops the class over-applying the rule, and it will be over-applied by Thursday without it.** **A long subject made of old information is easier to read than a short one made of new**, and a student who has learnt _no long subjects_ will start breaking up sentences that were never a problem. **The test is the reader\'s memory, not the word count.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _That the roller was out of service and that the pour went ahead are both recorded in the site diary._ → **fourteen words.** ⚠ **Extraposition:** _It is recorded in the site diary that the roller was out of service and that the pour went ahead._',
  '**2** ⚠ **No repair.** _The eight for whom no reason was recorded are the group this committee has to decide about._ **Eleven words of subject and all of it given.**',
  '**3** _That nobody asked for the service log for eleven weeks is the fact the insurer will be asked about._ → **thirteen words.** **Full stops:** _Nobody asked for the service log for eleven weeks. That is the fact the insurer will be asked about._',
  '**4** ⚠ **No repair.** _The nineteen who were turned away between January and June are two-thirds of a problem nobody has costed._',
  '**5** _How the figure was arrived at, who supplied it and when it was last checked are not stated anywhere._ → ⚠ **colon and list:** _Three things are not stated anywhere: how the figure was arrived at, who supplied it, and when it was last checked._',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('The word count before each verb, and the repair. ⚠ **Rows 2 and 4 need no repair on either card and the class will repair them anyway.** **When they do, ask what was gained** — **nothing, and the sentence is a word longer.** **Then the rule in its correct form: the test is whether the material is new, not how many words there are.** **A fifteen-word subject made of old information is easier than a six-word subject made of new**, and that sentence should go in notebooks.'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**a** _**Original:** "That the box was signed for on 26 June, that the eleven transfer sheets are in the file and that nobody has seen it since are the three facts the committee has."_ **Twenty-seven words.**',
  '_**Extraposed:** It is a fact that the box was signed for on 26 June, that the eleven sheets are in the file and that nobody has seen it since._   _**Colon and list:** The committee has three facts: the box was signed for on 26 June; the eleven transfer sheets are in the file; nobody has seen the box since._   _**Full stops:** The box was signed for on 26 June. The eleven transfer sheets are in the file. Nobody has seen the box since._',
  '⚠ _**I am keeping the full stops.** The three are three facts and a reader can take them one at a time. **The colon version is second best and the extraposed one is the one I wrote first.**_',
  '**b** _(my partner, pen in hand)_ _Her mark fell at "and that nobody" — word nineteen._ ⚠ _On the full-stop version she made no mark at all and asked why I had not written it that way._',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Mr Shihabi brought a sentence from a planning decision: that the site had been used for storage since 2011, that no objection was received from the directorate, and that the access road is adopted were accepted by the inspector. Twenty-nine words before the verb. Mrs Tarabishi said she lost it at the second that, and everybody does, and it is not a difficult sentence — there is nothing in it a reader cannot follow, and they simply have to hold three things with no idea what is going to be done with them. Dr Qabbani said the repair is it was accepted that, and Mrs Khoury said the inspector accepted three things, with a colon, is the better repair and the one nobody reaches for: extraposition is the standard fix and a colon and a list is often the honest one. Mrs Oueidat said both, depending on whether the three things are a list or an argument — if the reader needs all three together, extrapose; if they are three separate findings, give them a colon and three lines and the reader can take them one at a time, because a list pretending to be a sentence is the commonest fault in a decision document. Mr Tayara asked what the it actually does, and Mrs Khoury said nothing, which is the point: it holds the subject position so that the verb can arrive on time, and the reader gets is not in dispute at word four and then knows what to do with everything after it. Mrs Ajouz said she wrote one of those long subjects in her examination and remembers building it, and Mrs Khoury said most people build them deliberately and they are a sign of effort — which is why they survive, because a twenty-nine-word subject looks like careful work and reads like a corridor. Mr Zahreddine asked whether it matters in opinions read aloud at hearings, and Mrs Oueidat said it matters more there and in exactly one way: a reader can go back and a listener cannot, and the person reading it aloud is usually not you, so they will also put the stress in the wrong place. Mr Attar asked whether there is a number, and Mrs Khoury said there is no number and there is a test — read it aloud and see whether you need a breath before the verb, and if you do, the reader needed one too. And Mr Shihabi said the thing he will take away is that the inspector wrote that sentence to sound careful: twenty-nine words of care, and the three findings in it are the ones everybody has argued about since, because nobody could hold all three.']));
out.push(A('⚠ **The sentence from the planning decision should be put on the board in full and read aloud by a student, slowly.** **Twenty-nine words, and the room will lose it at the second _that_, exactly as Mrs Tarabishi did.** **Then the finding that matters more than the repair: the inspector wrote it to sound careful**, and the three findings inside it are the ones everybody has argued about since, because nobody could hold all three. **Care that cannot be held is not care.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** A subject longer than its predicate. → **✓** count both halves aloud.   **✗** A list built into a subject. → **✓** give it a colon.   **✗** Extraposition for three separate findings. → **✓** they want taking one at a time.   **✗** Stressing the anticipatory _it_. → **✓** say it as though it were not there.   **✗** Breaking up a long subject made of given material. → **✓** nothing was gained.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** ⚠ **Three things: that the site has been used for storage since 2011, that the directorate entered no objection, and that the access road is adopted** — **given a colon and taken one at a time, not built into a subject.**   **2** **_It is not in dispute that the storage use is lawful now_**, ⚠ **and the sentence that follows it ends on _whether the lawfulness survives a change of operator_, which is the new thing and the one the desk will chase.**   **3** **He has not spoken to the operator and has not read the 2016 closure notice**; ⚠ **if that notice says anything other than a closure, the inspector\'s four lines go and so does the story.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
